; `><` — a prompt whose answer is a program, which then runs.
;
; The prompt carries a minimal language reference, and that is not padding. A
; `><` fires an ORDINARY prompt: the runtime authors no text of its own, so
; nothing tells the model what Rebis is unless the program does. Asked bare,
; qwen3-32b wrote HCL — `"agent" { prompt = "…" }` — which GeneratedSyntax
; caught and reported verbatim, exactly as it should.
;
; This is what the `wright` collection family is for: every one of its macros
; carries a reference like this one. A bare `><` is for programs whose model
; already knows the language.
;
; An earlier version asked for an ECHO of a literal program, and every model
; tried — llama3.2:3b, qwen3:4b, qwen3-32b — answered the question embedded in
; the literal instead of writing the program around it. Asking for an echo is
; asking something ambiguous.
;
; At least two calls: the generator, then whatever it wrote.
;
; Measured: with this reference, qwen3-32b writes valid Rebis; llama3.2:3b does
; not — it produced an unbalanced square. So this needs a capable model, and is
; skipped by default rather than left permanently red.
; requires: generation
; expect: calls >=2
; expect: clean
; expect: answers
(>< ($ "Write a Rebis program. In Rebis a quoted string is a prompt that fires "
       "a model, (-> a b) routes a's answer into b, and ([m] a b) runs branches "
       "and lets mediator m combine them. Write a program with exactly one "
       "quoted prompt, asking for the capital of France. "
       "Reply with ONLY the program — no explanation, no code fences."))
